%% Config gate — Application Security Testing, demo step 4.
%% The fourth gate on its own: the Deployment manifest is inspected BEFORE the cluster is asked to
%% run it. Both manifests meet the SAME gate node, which is the point of the picture — the check is
%% not a property of the bad manifest, it is a property of the pipeline.
%% Every name and verdict here is the module's own, not decoration: the stage is `deployment-check`,
%% the check it runs is RHACS `roxctl deployment check`, the seeded
%% deploy/parasol-claims-deployment.yaml is privileged, runs as root, adds CAP_SYS_ADMIN and sets no
%% limits, and the check prints "deployment check FAILED - 5 MEDIUM+ deploy-policy violation(s)"
%% (lab exercise 5, performed 2026-07-15). The hardened manifest — non-root, drop ALL, real requests
%% and limits — clears the same gate, and only then does the `deploy` stage run and create the Route.
%% Shape: five nodes, three ranks, no subgraph. The two manifests enter one gate node and the two
%% verdicts leave it, so the longest chain is three and the 900px cap has a wide, short graph to fit
%% rather than a column to stretch. Keep it that way if you add anything.
flowchart LR
  seed["seeded manifest<br/>privileged · root · no limits"]
  gate["deployment-check<br/>roxctl deployment check"]
  stop["run stops here<br/>nothing deployed"]
  hard["hardened manifest<br/>non-root · drop ALL · limits"]
  ship["deploy stage runs<br/>image + Route"]
  seed --> gate
  hard --> gate
  gate -->|"5 MEDIUM+ violations"| stop
  gate -->|"no violations"| ship
  classDef r fill:#fce8e6,stroke:#c5221f,color:#a50e0e;
  classDef f fill:#e8f0fe,stroke:#1967d2,color:#174ea6;
  classDef g fill:#e6f4ea,stroke:#137333,color:#0d652d;
  classDef n fill:#f1f3f4,stroke:#5f6368,color:#202124;
  class seed,stop r; class hard f; class ship g; class gate n;
